= Context

Floods are deadly and costly: every year, they cause thousands of deaths, the displacement of millions of people and hundreds of billions of dollars in damage, and climate change is expected to increase both their frequency and intensity @fox2024. They are driven by many phenomena such as sea level rise, extreme precipitation, or excessive snowmelt, which anthropogenic climate change affects significantly @ipcc. While still somewhat uncertain, floods' occurrences are predicted to diminish in some parts of the world, e.g. in some parts of Europe @bloschl2019changing, while increasing in others, like Asia @Wang2021SpatiotemporalDO --- where 90\% of flood-affected people live @Merz2021CausesIA --- as well as tropical Africa and South America @ipcc. About 23\% of the world's population, some 1.8 billion people, is directly exposed to a 100-year flood @najafi2024, and exposure is greatest in low- and middle-income countries @fox2024. In these comparatively poorer regions, the flood mortality is the highest, both in rates and in absolute numbers, and income inequality is associated with more flood fatalities @Lindersson2023TheWT. The global death toll is then expected to rise, and current improvements in mitigation strategies are not yet enough to prevent it @Hu2018FloodinducedMA, while exposure is expected to increase with rapid demographic change @fox2024. In middle- and high-income countries, major flood disasters have generally not become less fatal since 1990 @Lindersson2023TheWT.

The first step in decreasing the negative impact of flooding on anthropogenic activities is to develop a thorough understanding of its direct and indirect consequences. Indeed, precise comprehension of the aftermaths of such disasters is necessary in order to correctly assess the effectiveness of protection measures, socio-economic policies or land-use planning aimed at building flood resilience @Allaire2018SocioeconomicIO.

#pagebreak()

Evaluating the effects of flooding is a wide-ranging topic that covers many fields of research and operational entities. It involves both public and private organizations, which all look at the issue from their own unique and different perspectives. In this technical report we propose ours, which is of course biased in its own way. The goal of this document is primarily technical, but it is worth situating the work before diving in. Without being fully exhaustive of the existing literature, this chapter should at least provide a compendious view of the matter at hand and give insights into the methods and experiments developed further in this report.

This chapter first reviews what floods are, how they are modelled and observed, and how their impacts are assessed. Chapter II presents the impact estimation pipeline developed in this work, which turns satellite imagery or a hydrodynamic simulation into an estimate of the direct economic and social impacts of a flood. Chapter III presents an agent-based model that explores how social damage curves could be derived from simulated behaviour. Chapter IV discusses the limits of the approach and the perspectives it opens.

