== Flood types and origins

#rotate(270deg, reflow: true)[
  #figure(caption: [Hydrological cycle (USGS, 2022)])[
    #image("../imgs/water_cycle_usgs.png", width: 94%)
  ] <fig:water_cycle_usgs>
]

Floods occur when, in the simplest terms, there is water where it should not be, more specifically, temporary water on usually dry land. Water being a ubiquitous component of Earth and human activities, floods can take many shapes @VIGLIONE20153 @Opolot2013ApplicationOR. The hydrological cycle shown in @fig:water_cycle_usgs, from the United States Geological Survey (USGS), illustrates the processes that lead to floods rather than the flood types themselves. Floods are summarized here in three main types, even though reality is often more complex.

/ River (fluvial) or overflowing floods: they happen when the water input of a river system is abnormally high, due to increased precipitation, snowmelt, rising groundwater, or flow blockages. Water gradually builds up until it reaches the flooding threshold. The process is slow, can last weeks or months, and can typically be predicted in advance.

/ Flash (pluvial) floods: happen when there is a sudden massive input of water, such as from heavy localized precipitation, dike or dam breaches, or glacial lake outburst floods (GLOFs). They can also occur outside of river systems, for example when heavy rainfall produces landslides. They typically occur in small catchments, particularly in areas with steep terrain, low permeability soils, or impervious surfaces. Their rapid rise, high flow velocities, and extremely short warning times make them the most dangerous to human life.

/ Coastal floods: happen when water is pushed from the sea or large lakes onto the land, for example because of storm surges from wind storms and low atmospheric pressure, or gradual sea level rise caused by melting ice caps due to climate change. A key characteristic of coastal floods is that water levels fluctuate with the tide, and the intrusion of saltwater causes specific damages such as soil and groundwater salinization.

Depending on the location and meteorological conditions, floods can often be combined: coastal, pluvial and fluvial mechanisms can coincide or follow one another in close succession, and such compound floods pose a level of threat much greater than what each mechanism is capable of in isolation @Jafarzadegan2023RecentAA.

