== Hydrological modelling

Flood hazard in space and time is most often characterized with numerical models. The reference approach relies on hydrodynamic models, which numerically solve derivations of the Navier–Stokes equations, principally the Shallow Water Equations (SWEs), to simulate the motion of water in channels and floodplains and to estimate the spatiotemporal distribution of water depth @Jafarzadegan2023RecentAA. Flood inundation is typically computed with one-dimensional (1D), two-dimensional (2D), or coupled 1D/2D models. Although 2D models represent floodplain topography more realistically and generally outperform 1D models, they are computationally expensive; recent numerical developments have therefore focused on efficiency, notably by solving reduced forms of the SWEs, adopting hybrid 1D/2D schemes, and exploiting high-performance computing and Graphics Processing Units (GPUs) @Jafarzadegan2023RecentAA @VIGLIONE20153. Such models are set up with terrain data, channel and floodplain roughness coefficients, boundary conditions given by flow or water-level hydrographs, and initial conditions @Jafarzadegan2023RecentAA, through the hydrodynamic expertise of dedicated scientists and engineers. Beyond careful setup, their precision can be further improved through data assimilation, in which model predictions are combined with observations, such as satellite-derived flood extents and water levels, to correct the model state and parameters @Nguyen2022ImprovementOF. Because hydrodynamic models are demanding in terms of data and computing time, simplified methods have been developed as alternatives. Topography-based approaches, such as Height Above Nearest Drainage (HAND) @Nobre2011HeightAT, the Teng–Vaze–Dutta method (TVD) @Teng2013SimplifiedMF, and the Floodwater Depth Estimation Tool (FwDET) @Cohen2018EstimatingFD, estimate floodwater depth directly from a remote-sensing water extent and a digital elevation model (DEM) @Teng2022ACA. They are computationally efficient and well suited to rapid, continental-scale mapping, but they are less accurate than hydrodynamic models; the remote sensing section below describes how they work and where they fail. Alternatively, data-driven and deep learning surrogates can emulate hydrodynamic simulations at a fraction of their cost @Bentivoglio2021DeepLM @vu2025high, and data assimilation of satellite-derived flood maps has been used to improve the parameterization of large-scale hydraulic models @ayoub2022assimilation, that can replace SWEs solvers has cheaper alternatives. These modelled flood characteristics ultimately feed into flood risk assessments, for example to estimate potential loss of life @Ge2022EstimatingLO or to quantify the socio-economic impact of river floods @alfieri2016modelling, with the level of detail of such analyses depending on the decision at hand @sieg2023toward.


== Remote sensing

Flood mapping from space has been feasible since the 1970s, and many studies have confirmed that it can reach good accuracy @ayoub2022assimilation. Both optical and microwave sensors can be used, and they detect water differently. Optical sensors measure the sunlight reflected by the ground, and water stands out because it absorbs strongly in the Near Infrared (NIR) and beyond @rahman2017state; indices such as the Modified Normalized Difference Water Index (MNDWI) are commonly used to map the extent of flood inundation @sajjad2022riverine. Radar sensors are active, and among satellite Earth Observation (EO) sensors, Synthetic Aperture Radar (SAR) sensors are usually the first choice for flood mapping: they acquire images by day and night, in all weather conditions, and smooth open water is straightforward to detect @ayoub2022assimilation, as water reflects microwave radiation specularly, most of the energy sent by the sensor never returns to the antenna and water appears dark in radar images @rahman2017state.

The mapping methods themselves evolved from simple visual interpretation and backscatter thresholding @rahman2017state to machine and deep learning, where models based on convolutional layers are usually more accurate because their inductive biases suit the spatial structure of flood events @Bentivoglio2021DeepLM, for example U-Net architectures to detect water bodies @de2025highly. More broadly, remote sensing and Geographic Information System (GIS) techniques are critical at every stage of flood management, from prediction, prevention and mitigation to flood risk identification and damage assessment, and they give cheaper and faster access to spatial data on the flood event, including in areas that are physically inaccessible @Opolot2013ApplicationOR.

Several open tools support these workflows: S1Tiling @s1tilling allows downloading and normalizing Sentinel-1 data on the Sentinel-2 tiles; FloodML @floodml converts satellite imagery into binary flood masks using a Random Forest; and Bulldozer @bulldozer2023 extracts digital terrain models (DTMs) from digital surface models (DSMs); all three of them were developed at the Centre national d'études spatiales (CNES). FloodML was notably used in the Space Climate Observatory (SCO) projects FloodDAM @flooddam and FloodDAM-DT @flooddam-dt that are closely linked to the context of this technical report.

#pagebreak()

Satellite-based products are promising for at least three reasons: the *rapidity*, *globalization*, and *openness* of the method, which are central to the work conducted in the report and the design choices that went in the tools presented here.

In the aftermath of natural disasters, the first few hours and days are crucial. Decisions about where and how much help to send are informed by key information about the scale of the disaster and the areas affected. Satellite imagery is already being used for this purpose and has proven its usefulness with services such as Copernicus Emergency Management Service (CEMS) and the International Charter: Space and Major Disasters @cemsmapping @charter, which provide quick information on the number of people affected and the extent of the affected area anywhere on the globe, merely hours after an event @martinis2021.

Besides rapidity, globalization --- that is, the spatial exhaustiveness of spaceborne remote sensing --- is one of its most prominent strengths. Historically, flood behaviour and impacts have been studied through in-situ observations and hydrological modelling. This approach produces flood reanalyses and predictions of very high quality and resolution, provided there is the necessary expertise, public funding and computing time. However, these resources are distributed unevenly, meaning some regions, which are often highly flood-prone, do not have access to them. In such regions, the lack of data and the inability to quickly assess the situation after a flood event can result in unnecessary loss of life due to inadequate assistance @HO2021131.

Openness, on the other hand, is not guaranteed in data-rich regions: data is often gathered quickly after an event, but could remain unavailable if kept behind closed doors, such as those of insurance companies. To make a profit, contract premiums and deductibles are carefully adjusted using insights from the aftermath of events. Quality assessment data is difficult to find in open access because it forms the core of their business model — it is reasonable to assume that they do not want their competitors to access it, but this also makes it harder for scientists and local government bodies to develop damage models based on accurate information. However, with the continuously increasing spatial resolution of openly available satellite imagery and automatic asset mapping techniques @SENOLCABI2021165, it is now easier than ever to have a rough estimate of the economic values of destroyed assets, as well as population density.

However, spaceborne remote sensing also has major drawbacks. Optical sensors are blocked by clouds, and the sky is often overcast precisely when the information is most needed, so the most useful acquisitions may simply be unavailable @sajjad2022riverine. Revisit times also limit the ability to capture a flood event: the revisit cycle of most moderate-resolution optical platforms exceeds ten days, and the gap between the flood peak and the satellite overpass can make the resulting map miss most of the flooded area @rahman2017state. Flash floods are especially challenging, as their peak devastation lasts only a few hours @rahman2017state. In built-up areas, distinguishing water from paved surfaces is not straightforward, since asphalt and water both reflect very little NIR energy @rahman2017state, leading to uncertainty in urban SAR images of flooding.

Beyond these limits, spaceborne flood mapping only provides the extent of a flood, which alone is not enough for impact assessment: such products give the areal extent of an event efficiently and accurately, but they do not give the floodwater depth, which first responders and damage assessment need @Cohen2018EstimatingFD. Water depth must then be estimated by combining the flood extent with a DEM, with topography-based methods such as HAND, TVD or FwDET @Teng2022ACA. These methods remain approximate: FwDET gets the edges right but loses accuracy away from the inundation boundary, where it has to interpolate to reach the greater depths @Teng2022ACA; it is meant for a first-order overview rather than a detailed analysis of floodwater dynamics @Cohen2018EstimatingFD, and these techniques have not been tested rigorously enough on out-of-sample events that differ from those they were built on @Jafarzadegan2023RecentAA. More recent tools aim at improving the depth estimation from flood extents: FLEXTH uses simple morphological arguments to expand the flooded area into excluded regions and derives water depths from the terrain elevation at the boundary between flooded and dry areas @Betterle2024WaterDE, while Inflos interpolates the water surface elevation from sample points along the flooded boundary, taken from satellite imagery, and was designed for the Rapid Mapping component of CEMS @Poterek2025INterpolatedFS.

