== Artificial intelligence

Artificial intelligence (AI), and more specifically Machine and Deep Learning (ML/DL), refer to algorithms and statistical models that perform a task without explicit instructions, relying on patterns and inference instead @Wang2021AIforFlood. It is ubiquitous, (often for good reasons, even if the drawbacks are to be mentioned) and floods are not exempt from it.


In flood management, DL techniques are increasingly used to overcome the computational burden of slow numerical models and to improve traditional flood mapping methods @Bentivoglio2021DeepLM: these methods primarily address three mapping applications, namely flood inundation mapping to delineate flooded areas from satellite or aerial imagery, flood susceptibility mapping to categorize flood-prone areas from environmental factors, and flood hazard mapping to estimate water depths. In remote sensing, machine learning is mostly used to turn satellite images into flood maps: a single image is only a snapshot of the land surface and does not describe the whole flood process, so machine learning learns the relation between input images and flood extent from examples @Zare2021MLsatellite. Beyond mapping, machine learning is also used to predict hydrological variables such as river discharge and flood levels, and to assess flood risk @Casado2021MLRS. AI is not limited to satellite imagery, as it is also applied to crowdsourced data, social media and surveillance cameras @Wang2021AIforFlood, although the quality of these unconventional data remains difficult to control, which limits the transfer of the methods from one place to another @Wang2021AIforFlood.

For hazard mapping in particular, DL models serve as fast surrogate models for numerical hydrodynamic simulations, providing significant speed-ups --- up to several orders of magnitude --- while maintaining reasonable accuracy @Bentivoglio2021DeepLM @Jafarzadegan2023RecentAA. Such a surrogate is trained on the results of a high-fidelity hydrodynamic model and emulates its response at a much lower cost @Jafarzadegan2023RecentAA. Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) are the most common architecture for spatial flood analysis, while Recurrent Neural Networks (RNNs) are mainly used to model temporally varying floods; both benefit from their spatial and temporal inductive biases @Bentivoglio2021DeepLM. However, several challenges remain. Most existing models struggle to generalize to unseen case studies and cannot easily incorporate complex hydraulic structures or flood defenses, and they need large amounts of training data, which are difficult to collect in many regions @Bentivoglio2021DeepLM. 

In addition, most models have limited consideration for prediction uncertainties, and their black-box nature means that their use in critical situations should be validated against traditional models and expert knowledge @Bentivoglio2021DeepLM. To address these limitations, promising research directions include Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) and neural operators, Physics-Informed Neural Networks (PINNs) to enforce the governing physical equations, and probabilistic methods such as Bayesian neural networks to quantify uncertainties @Bentivoglio2021DeepLM @Jafarzadegan2023RecentAA.

Finally, as always with ML/DL techniques, the model can only be as good as its input data, which can already be scarce when trying to build a flood model, and even rarer when trying to understand its consequences: while simulations can provide potentially limitless data, observed data depend on the study area and water depth is only measured where gauging stations exist @Bentivoglio2021DeepLM, and records of past damage are often geographically scattered or too general to be turned into data that can train or validate a damage model @huizinga2017global.

