== Conclusion

This chapter reviewed the pieces that this report assembles. Floods come in three main types, river, flash and coastal, and they often combine, which makes the hazard hard to characterize with a single model. The reference way to characterize it is hydrodynamic modelling, which solves the shallow water equations but demands data, expertise and computing time; simplified topography-based methods and, more recently, machine learning surrogates offer cheaper alternatives, at the cost of accuracy and generalization. Satellite imagery brings the rapidity, spatial exhaustiveness and openness that in-situ networks and national models cannot guarantee everywhere, but it only gives the extent of an event, and the depth still has to be inferred from a digital elevation model.

On the impact side, direct damage is estimated with depth-damage curves, whose global version from the Joint Research Centre is a pragmatic compromise between consistency and local relevance, while social impacts are still mostly approached through population counts. The remaining uncertainty is concentrated in two places: how the hazard is represented by the model, and how socio-economic damage is inferred from it.

The goal of the following chapters is therefore to quantify these impacts by working in a narrow spatiotemporal niche: anywhere on the globe, quickly, and with open data.
